---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import SalesTaxCalculator from '../../components/SalesTaxCalculator.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="US Sales Tax Calculator" description="Calculate sales tax and total price for common US state base rates, or enter a custom rate.">
  <SalesTaxCalculator client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why "the sales tax rate" isn't a single number</h2>
  <p>US sales tax is genuinely fragmented — state base rates are just the starting point, with many cities and counties layering additional local tax on top, meaning the actual rate charged at checkout can vary meaningfully even within the same state.</p>

  <h2>How to use this calculator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Enter the purchase amount.</li>
    <li>Select a state for its base rate, or enter a custom rate for your specific locality.</li>
    <li>See the tax amount and total price.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Example</h2>
  <p>A $100 purchase in a state with a 7.25% base rate adds $7.25 in tax for a $107.25 total — before any additional local city/county tax that might apply.</p>

  <h2>Common Sales Tax Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Using only the state base rate</strong> when local tax significantly changes the effective rate in many cities and counties.</li>
    <li><strong>Assuming all products are taxed the same</strong> — many states exempt or reduce tax on specific categories like groceries or clothing.</li>
    <li><strong>Not verifying current rates</strong> — tax rates change periodically and should be confirmed against official state resources for anything beyond a rough estimate.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use This Sales Tax Calculator</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Getting a quick estimate of total cost including tax for a purchase</li>
    <li>Comparing base sales tax rates across different states</li>
    <li>Calculating tax for an invoice using a known local rate</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Sales Tax FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Why doesn't my actual receipt match this calculation?</strong> Local city/county taxes on top of the state base rate are extremely common in the US — check your specific city's combined rate for an exact match.</p>
</ToolLayout>
